Elisabeth Doisneau ; préf. de Alain Abelhauser.  aRennes :bPresses universitaires de Rennes,c1999.  a249 p. ;c24 cm.  aFonds Yves Hébert  aReprenant le titre d'un des articles majeurs des écrits de Lacan, S. Zizek a pour ambition de prendre au sérieux le concept de sujet, tel que la psychanalyse peut le promouvoir, en y reconnaissant une dimension de subversion essentielle. Puis, nanti de cette boussole, il revient aux champs de la philosophie et du politique, en la mettant à l'épreuve de cette subversion. [Memento] 6aSujet (psychologie) 6aIdées politiques99053 6aPsychanalyse et philosophie936159  aClinique, psychanalytique et psychopathologie.  bAnnabel Labelle  cLIVRE2ddc  00104070aCaQJCbCaQJCcLANAU.CGd2014-04-16l1o150.195 Z82bp002072126r2024-03-27 13:56:52s2024-02-27w2014-04-16yLIVRE  c335711d33571101361nam a2200313 a 4500952013200000999001900132003000600151005001700157008004100174020001800215024001800233040001500251041000800266044000700274082001900281100003600300245003400336260002800370300002100398490001500419504005000434520041400484650002700898650002200925830004000947942001500987500002301002901002201025  00104070aCaQJCbCaQJCcLANAU.CGd2014-04-16l2o199.4973 Z82pp002072134r2024-10-22 08:00:16s2024-09-24w2014-04-16yLIVRE  c335721d335721CaQJC20250401101630.0140416s2008    fr            000 0 fre d  a97822136366583 a9782213636658  aCaQJCbfre0 afre  afr0 a199.4973bZ82p1 aŽižek, Slavoj,d1949-931517113aLa parallaxe /cSlavoj Zizek.  aParis :bFayard,c2008.  a458 p. ;c24 cm.1 aOuvertures  aNotes (partiellement bibliogr.) : p. 417-458.  aLa parallaxe est l'apparent déplacement d'un objet causé par un changement du point d'observation et offrant une nouvelle ligne de vision. A travers une relecture de Kant, Hegel, Kierkegaard, Heidegger, cet ouvrage analyse les différents modes de cette coupure, philosophique, théologique, scientifique et politique, en vue d'une refondation du matérialisme dialectique et du projet communiste. [Memento] 6aParallaxexPhilosophie 6aPhilosophie91795 0aOuvertures (Fayard (Firme))9276171  2ddccLIVRE  aFonds Yves Hébert  bAmélie C. Lepage01237cam a2200265 a 4500952012800000999001900128001001100147003000600158005001700164008004100181020001800222040002000240041000800260044000700268082001900275100004300294245007100337260005600408300002900464520033700493586003400830650004000864901005200904942001500956  00104070aCaQJCbCaQJCcLANAU.RLEAd2014-04-22g9.99l0o813.54  M996mp002079631r2024-06-14 00:00:00w2014-04-22yLIVRE  c335931d3359310003045250CaQJC20210324132812.0980923s1999    nyua   c      000 f eng    a9780064407311  aDLCdCaQJCbfre0 aeng  any0 a813.54 bM996m  aMyers, Walter Dean,d1937-2014936063010aMonster /cWalter Dean Myers ; illustrations by Christopher Myers.  aNew York, N.Y. :bHarperCollins Publishers,cc1999.  a281 p. :bill. ;c22 cm.  aThis New York Times bestselling novel from acclaimed author Walter Dean Myers tells the story of Steve Harmon, a teenage boy in juvenile detention and on trial. Presented as a screenplay of Steve's own imagination, and peppered with journal entries, the book shows how one single decision can change our whole lives. [HarperCollins]  aMichael L. Printz Award, 2000 694635aRoman américainy20e siècle  aAL (22-04-14)bStéfany-Cristina Ocampo-Brodeur  2ddccLIVRE02040cam a2200313 a 4500952014200000952014500142999001900287001001000306003000600316005001700322008004100339020001800380020001700398040002200415041000800437044000700445082001900452100003600471245007200507246001200579260003900591300002600630520087700656650004201533650006001575650003801635901003801673942001501711  00104070aCaQJCbCaQJCcLANAU.RLEAd2014-04-22g13.75l1o843.914  W651np002079657r2024-06-14 00:00:00s2015-01-29w2014-04-22yLIVRE  00104070aCaQJCbCaQJCcLANAU.RLEAd2017-04-27l1o843.914 W651n ex.2p002163155r2024-06-14 00:00:00s2017-11-09tex.2w2017-04-27yLIVRE  c335941d335941030586727CaQJC20250218125901.0060105s2006    nyu           000 0 eng d  a9780374399979  a978374500016  aTXBXLdCaQJCbfre0 aeng  any0 a843.914bW651n1 aWiesel, Elie,d1928-201692319110aNight /cElie Wiesel ; translated from the French by Marion Wiesel.03aLa Nuit  aNew York :bHill and Wang,cc2006.  axxi, 120 p. ;c22 cm.  aNight is Elie Wiesel's masterpiece, a candid, horrific, and deeply poignant autobiographical account of his survival as a teenager in the Nazi death camps. This new translation by Marion Wiesel, Elie's wife and frequent translator, presents this seminal memoir in the language and spirit truest to the author's original intent. And in a substantive new preface, Elie reflects on the enduring importance of Night and his lifelong, passionate dedication to ensuring that the world never forgets man's capacity for inhumanity to man.  Night offers much more than a litany of the daily terrors, everyday perversions, and rampant sadism at Auschwitz and Buchenwald; it also eloquently addresses many of the philosophical as well as personal questions implicit in any serious consideration of what the Holocaust was, what it meant, and what its legacy is and will be. [AbeBooks] 6942312aJuifsvRomans, nouvelles, etc. 69140800aCamps de concentrationvRomans, nouvelles, etc. 69769aRoman françaisy20e siècle  aAL (22-04-14)bOcéane Blanchette  2ddccLIVRE01538nam a2200313 i 450000100060000000300060000600500170001200800510002902000180008004000330009804100080013104400080013908200250014710000340017224501730020625000160037926000730039530000200046850400330048852003470052161000860086865000550095465000520100970000350106171000590109690100350115594200150119099900190120559751CaQJC20250430153027.0140417s2014    qucŒŒŒŒf |ŒŒŒ 0ŒŒ Œ fre c  a9782892296143  aCaQSFCEbfrecCaQSFCEdCaQJC1 afre  aquc0 a610.73076bO65g 2014  aLessard, Louise-Marie911303910aGuid